                <span class="badge-progress">in progress</span>
                <p class="hero-label">Aerospace · Propulsion &amp; Controls</p>
                <h1>Thrust Vector<br />Control Rocket</h1>
                <p class="hero-desc">
                    Building a 4"-diameter model rocket with an active
                    thrust-vector-control system, aiming toward a fully
                    propulsive vertical landing — the Falcon 9 booster
                    concept, built at hobbyist scale from COTS and
                    3D-printed parts. A Teensy 4.1 flight computer drives a
                    two-axis gimbaled motor mount through a PID loop reading
                    attitude from an onboard IMU.
                </p>
                <div class="skill-tags">
                    <span class="skill-tag">Python</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">PID Control</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">Monte Carlo Simulation</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">Siemens NX</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">NXOpen / Python</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">Embedded (Teensy 4.1)</span>
                    <span class="skill-tag">OpenRocket</span>
                </div>
            </section>

            <p class="section-label">Problem / Goal</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    The goal is a fully propulsive vertical landing at
                    hobbyist scale — the same concept as a Falcon 9 booster
                    return, built from commercial-off-the-shelf and
                    3D-printed parts rather than an orbital-class vehicle.
                    That means a flight computer that can read the rocket's
                    attitude in real time and actively correct it through a
                    gimbaled motor mount, rather than relying on fins for
                    passive stability.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The long-term architecture uses two solid motors stacked
                    in series rather than a throttleable engine: an ascent
                    motor for launch and a separate landing motor for the
                    descent burn. Because the motors are fixed-thrust,
                    control comes from <em>timing</em> — the flight computer
                    has to calculate the ignition altitude for the landing
                    burn dynamically at apogee — rather than from
                    throttling. The project is informed by an existence
                    proof: a hobbyist ("Project Horizon") flew a rocket
                    called Eagle using the same motor class and the same
                    flight-computer architecture (Teensy 4.1) and achieved a
                    successful propulsive landing after 30 iterative
                    attempts.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Approach &amp; Key Decisions</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    <strong>Simulation, built in layers.</strong> Before any
                    hardware design, I modeled descent physics (gravity,
                    drag, IMU double-integration for altitude) to answer one
                    question: after the flight computer detects the ignition
                    altitude, is there enough time before ground impact to
                    fire the landing motor and get meaningful thrust out of
                    it? That meant building a full latency budget — sensor
                    loop cycle, MOSFET switching time, e-match ignition
                    delay, motor spool-up time — and subtracting it from the
                    physical time-to-impact. That budget turned out to be the
                    single constraint the rest of the vehicle design has to
                    satisfy, and it set starting values for apogee altitude
                    and detection altitude before any CAD existed.
                </p>
                <p>
                    From there, a control law: sweeping possible apogee
                    altitudes and solving for the minimum safe ignition
                    altitude at each one produces a linear fit,
                    h_b = m·Ap + c, that runs as firmware — every flight
                    measures its own apogee and computes its own ignition
                    altitude, rather than flying to a fixed number. A full 2D
                    pitch-plane flight sim (<code>tvc_sim.py</code>) then
                    modeled the digitized F15 motor thrust curve, time-varying
                    mass/CG/inertia as propellant burns and motors eject,
                    finless aerodynamics, and gimbaled-thrust dynamics
                    through the full ascent → coast → descent → landing-burn
                    → touchdown sequence, running the same
                    PID-with-thrust-feed-forward controller that's meant to
                    fly onboard.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A stochastic layer on top
                    (<code>tvc_sim_stochastic.py</code>,
                    <code>tvc_sim_sweeps.py</code>) added IMU
                    accelerometer/gyro noise and bias, a strapdown
                    attitude/altitude estimator running at realistic sensor
                    rates (1 kHz IMU, 100 Hz control loop, optional
                    barometer fusion), servo dynamics, crosswind and gusts,
                    and motor-to-motor impulse scatter — turning hundreds of
                    randomized flights into a distribution of touchdown
                    velocities instead of one idealized answer. Parameter
                    sweeps showed touchdown velocity is far more sensitive to
                    IMU accelerometer bias than to servo speed, which
                    redirected effort toward sensor calibration over faster
                    servos.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Design trade-offs.</strong> An early
                    multi-perspective design review (five independent
                    analyses plus cross-review, synthesized into one brief)
                    set the core architecture: shared gimbal and PID/TVC code
                    for both ascent and landing burns, a low center of
                    gravity for passive self-righting during unpowered
                    descent, a dynamic (not fixed) ignition-altitude law,
                    legs sized for off-nominal landing velocities rather than
                    just the nominal case, and a five-state flight software
                    model (boost → coast → descent → ignition → landing). A
                    4" commercial airframe and nose cone were chosen
                    specifically to avoid needing CFD — known, published
                    aerodynamics. Because the vehicle is finless and relies
                    on active TVC, the traditional "positive stability
                    margin" goal doesn't apply the way it would to a normal
                    model rocket; the real requirement is a torque budget
                    where gimbal-deflected thrust can out-torque the
                    aerodynamic destabilizing moment through the burn.
                    CG/CP modeling is being done in OpenRocket, with the
                    first pass run using placeholder masses pending final
                    hardware weigh-in.
                </p>
                <p>
                    A launch-off-the-legs plan was dropped once the low-CG
                    design was confirmed passively unstable during
                    ascent — the same property that helps it self-right on
                    descent works against it going up — in favor of a
                    lug/rail launcher, sized after determining the rocket's
                    mass and hardware likely put it past the point where a
                    thin launch rod would be adequate ("rod whip" risk).
                    Recovery iterated toward a servo-actuated nose-cone
                    separation: a servo retracts a hook/pin, a compression
                    spring provides positive separation, and the nose cone
                    stays tethered by a Kevlar shock cord through a
                    ball-bearing swivel (to prevent parachute twist) with the
                    chute packed in-line. Motor staging reuses a friction-fit,
                    e-match-sandwiched coupling from the reference build: the
                    ascent motor's own thrust holds it against the landing
                    motor during boost, and the landing motor's ignition
                    ejects the spent casing — no separate mechanical release
                    needed.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <strong>Scope discipline.</strong> The first flight is
                    deliberately scoped down to TVC-controlled ascent with
                    parachute recovery only, deferring the two-motor
                    propulsive landing stack and legs to a later flight once
                    ascent control is proven on real flight data. That
                    follows from a testing philosophy of treating every
                    flight as a data-collection event rather than a
                    pass/fail attempt, and maximizing what can be validated
                    on the bench — static fires, hardware-in-the-loop
                    state-machine testing, drop tests for leg deployment —
                    before committing to a full-system flight.
                </p>
            </div>

            <p class="section-label">Results / Data</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    Simulation results so far: the Monte Carlo sweeps
                    established that touchdown velocity is dominated by IMU
                    accelerometer bias rather than servo response speed —
                    a concrete result that changed where engineering effort
                    is going (sensor calibration and fusion, not faster
                    actuators).
                </p>
                <p>
                    On the hardware side, mechanical design is done in
                    Siemens NX with STEP/STL export for 3D printing. Parts
                    modeled and iterated so far include the TVC gimbal
                    assembly (inner ring, outer ring, and arm components
                    across multiple versions), a motor container currently
                    being redesigned after the first version came out
                    oversized, a parametric tangent-ogive nose cone generated
                    from a scripted NXOpen (Python) journal so the geometry
                    can be regenerated instantly if the body-tube diameter or
                    ogive length changes, a full-vehicle model used to check
                    part fit and packaging, and an avionics bay that went
                    through a full redesign — from a two-sled,
                    threaded-rod-rail concept to a single rigid printed tray
                    (~88 × 130 mm) that mounts directly to the airframe wall,
                    laid out for the Teensy, IMU, barometer, both voltage
                    regulators, the power-distribution perfboard, and the
                    battery, with the IMU intentionally mounted rigidly
                    rather than vibration-isolated. CAD outputs feed directly
                    back into simulation: measured and estimated part
                    masses/dimensions are tracked in a parts-reference table
                    used to build the OpenRocket aerodynamic/stability model.
                </p>
                <p>
                    The gimbal itself is a dual-axis ring assembly that
                    holds the motor mount and allows independent rotation
                    about two perpendicular axes via pivot tabs sized for
                    servo linkages — a ring-within-a-ring layout that keeps
                    the motor centerline free while routing actuation loads
                    through the outer mounting tabs. Parts are 3D-printed to
                    iterate quickly on tolerances between the inner motor
                    mount and outer ring before committing to a final
                    material.
                </p>
