            <p class="section-label">Lessons Learned / What's Next</p>
            <div class="content-block">
                <p>
                    The Monte Carlo sweeps were the clearest lesson so far:
                    intuition said servo speed would dominate landing
                    performance, and the data said otherwise. Sweeping
                    parameters individually before committing engineering
                    time to any one subsystem is now part of how I'd approach
                    the next project, not just this one.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Open risks currently being tracked: motor thrust-to-weight
                    margin (vehicle mass has to stay within a fairly narrow
                    band for the selected Estes F15-0 motor to provide
                    adequate liftoff acceleration), a control-loop latency
                    failure mode inherited as a known risk from the reference
                    build, and whether the finished vehicle's weight or motor
                    impulse crosses from model-rocket into
                    high-power-rocketry classification, which would trigger
                    certification requirements. Next up is finishing the
                    avionics bay and motor container redesigns, closing out
                    the OpenRocket stability model with real (not
                    placeholder) part masses, and working toward the
                    descoped first flight — TVC-controlled ascent with
                    parachute recovery only — before attempting the full
                    two-motor propulsive landing sequence on a later flight.
                </p>
            </div>

